// Pollination decides how much fruit and seed a crop sets (2026-09-26,
// gardening depth). Read by src/systems/farming/pollination.rs, which carries
// the model; this file carries the crops, the numbers and where each came
// from. Every source below was read on 2026-09-26.
//
// WHAT THE GAME TEACHES. A crop whose harvest is a fruit or a seed only makes
// it from a pollinated flower. Outdoors the wind and wild insects do that
// work unnoticed. Indoors there is neither, so a tomato's pollen stays in its
// flower and a zucchini's never reaches the female flower: the plant grows
// and flowers normally and then sets little or nothing. The grower's answer
// is to pollinate by hand (shaking or buzzing the flowers, or carrying pollen
// with a brush) every few days while they flower, or to keep bumblebees,
// which is how commercial glasshouses do it. Leafy greens, roots and tubers
// are harvested without fruit, so pollination does not matter for them and
// they are not listed here; nor are the crops that pollinate themselves
// inside a closed flower, beyond the few listed to say so with a source.
//
// THE MODEL, in one paragraph (pollination.rs has the detail). A crop listed
// here with `no_help` below 1 keeps a record of the garden days it spent in
// its `flowering` stages and how many of those its flowers were pollinated:
// always in an outdoor field (farming::is_field_area), and indoors while a
// hand pollination still covers it (`hand_every_days` from the last one) or
// while a bumblebee hive reaches its grow area (Vibration and Insects crops
// only; bees do nothing for a wind-pollinated crop). At harvest its fruit set
// is no_help + (1 - no_help) x (pollinated days / flowering days), one
// multiplier on the yield, the way flowers opened while nobody pollinated
// them set at the unhelped rate and the rest set fully. A crop that never
// flowered on the clock (spawned ripe, ripened by the dev button, or carried
// past flowering by the offline catch-up) is not charged for flowers it
// never had.
//
// FIELDS
//   id              - a data/plants.csv id (a test checks each)
//   by              - how its flowers are pollinated:
//                       SelfPollinating: inside the flower, before or as it
//                         opens; nothing is needed indoors
//                       Vibration: self-fertile, but the pollen only falls
//                         when the flower is shaken (wind, a bumblebee's buzz,
//                         a hand vibrator)
//                       Insects: pollen must be carried to another flower
//                       Wind: pollen blown from the tassel to the silks
//   flowering       - its plants.csv growth stages while its flowers are open
//                     (a test checks each against the crop's stage list)
//   no_help         - the share of a full crop it sets indoors with no
//                     pollinator and no hand pollination, 0 to 1
//   hand_every_days - how many garden days one hand pollination covers: how
//                     often the grower must repeat it while the crop flowers
//   how             - how to do it by hand, told to the player
//
// hive_cover_m2: the greenhouse floor one bumblebee colony serves. It reaches
//   the indoor grow areas whose centre lies within the radius of a circle of
//   that area around the hive, sqrt(1000 / pi) = 17.8 m (58 ft), measured
//   across the floor. Ohio State (Runnion, below): "bumble bee colonies should
//   be placed in densities of 7-15 colonies per hectare", so one colony per
//   667 to 1,429 m2 (7,200 to 15,400 ft2); 1,000 m2 (10,800 ft2) is ten per
//   hectare, inside that range. Seeds of Diversity Canada's pepper page gives
//   more: "A single hive of bumble bees can pollinate 3000 square meters of
//   greenhouse sweet peppers." A colony does not last (colony_life_days,
//   below). And a small garden is not what the bees are sold for: LSU
//   (Hanna, below) warns "In smaller operations, however, bumblebee
//   pollination is not a good choice. At any given time, opened flowers are
//   too few in number to supply enough pollen for the foraging bees. As a
//   result, bees visit opened flowers repeatedly, destroying the protective
//   anther tube". The game does not model that harm; it is said on the
//   hive's card.
//
// colony_life_days: 70, the garden days one colony works after it is
//   introduced; then it is spent, its bees stop pollinating, the player is
//   told, and the hive does nothing until a new colony is introduced (the
//   Garden panel's Introduce colony button, which takes one colony_item). A
//   placed hive starts with no colony: the colony is what is bought, as a
//   grower buys it, and the hive box it comes in is the machine. 70 is ten
//   weeks, Koppert's instruction for every Natupol hive, including Natupol
//   Smart, the one for "small greenhouses up to 1000 m²" (the floor
//   hive_cover_m2 gives a hive here): "Remove bumblebee hives the latest 10
//   weeks after introduction". It sits at the short end of what the
//   extension and retail sources give: Runnion, "In commercially managed
//   hives, colonies generally last 10-14 weeks in a greenhouse or field
//   setting"; UF/IFAS (Hochmuth, below), "Hives are usually active for 6 to
//   10 weeks, and then must be replaced"; Arbico, of Koppert's Natupol
//   Excel, "lasts for about 9 weeks, but can go longer". Koppert also says
//   how it ends: "After some weeks the colony will reach its maximum size and
//   starts declining in size and pollination activity." The game does not
//   model that decline (no source gives its shape): a colony works fully for
//   its 70 days and then not at all.
//   THE CLOCK: garden days, the clock this file's flowering days and
//   hand_every_days already run on (the farming tick's days times the crop
//   growth speed), not world days. What a colony is worth is how much of a
//   crop's flowering it covers, and on the garden clock that share is the
//   same at every growth speed: ten weeks of the crops' own time, at 1x,
//   10x or 100x. Aged on the world clock, a colony at 100x growth would
//   outlive a hundred times as many crop seasons as a real one, and at 10x
//   ten times as many. (Which clock the body and the garden share is an
//   open question for the operator, docs/PRIORITIES.md, "Blocked on the
//   operator", item 3; this follows the garden's.) It ages only while the
//   game runs, like the rest of this file: the offline catch-up does not
//   age it, as it does not charge crops for flowers nobody pollinated while
//   the game was closed.
// colony_item: the bought item one colony is (data/items.csv, sold by the
//   vendor in data/trade_goods.ron, where its price and source are).
//
// devices: machines whose presence at a grow machine pollinates some of its
//   crops, as a hand or a bee would, by the share of a full crop each crop
//   sets under it (`sets`, 0 to 1; a crop not listed is not helped). A placed
//   device (a catalog machine of that `machine` key) serves the indoor grow
//   machine nearest it, and only while it is powered. See the entry.
//
// The sources for colony_life_days and devices, above, were read on
// 2026-09-27.
//
// SOURCES (cited by short name in the entries; the date after each is the
// source's own date):
//   McGregor: McGregor, S.E., "Insect Pollination of Cultivated Crop Plants",
//     USDA Agriculture Handbook 496, 1976; the online edition kept by the USDA
//     ARS Carl Hayden Bee Research Center (chapter pages captured 21 January
//     2009): https://www.ars.usda.gov/ARSUserFiles/20220500/OnlinePollinationHandbook.pdf
//   UMD Tunnels: Brust, G., "Problems with Pollination in High Tunnel
//     Tomatoes", University of Maryland Extension, 22 October 2024,
//     https://extension.umd.edu/resource/problems-pollination-high-tunnel-tomatoes
//   UMD Vegetables: "Pollination Problems of Vegetables", University of
//     Maryland Extension, updated 20 February 2023,
//     https://extension.umd.edu/resource/pollination-problems-vegetables
//   Abak: Abak, K., Sari, N., Paksoy, M., Kaftanoglu, O. and Yeninar, H.,
//     "Efficiency of bumble bees on the yield and quality of eggplant and
//     tomato grown in unheated glasshouses", Acta Horticulturae 412: 268-274,
//     1995, https://doi.org/10.17660/ActaHortic.1995.412.30
//   Klatt: Klatt, B.K. et al., "Bee pollination improves crop quality, shelf
//     life and commercial value", Proceedings of the Royal Society B 281:
//     20132440, 22 January 2014, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3866401/
//   UF/IFAS: Mallinger, R.E. and Liburd, O.E., "Pollination of Cucurbita spp.
//     (Squash and Pumpkin) Crops in Florida", ENY-2056/IN1311, University of
//     Florida IFAS Extension (no date shown on the page as read),
//     https://ask.ifas.ufl.edu/publication/IN1311
//   NDSU: Kandel, H., Endres, G. and Buetow, R. (eds.), "Sunflower Production
//     Guide", A1995, North Dakota State University Extension, December 2020,
//     https://www.ndsu.edu/agriculture/extension/publications/sunflower-production-guide
//   Purdue: Nielsen, R.L., "Silk Development and Emergence in Corn", Purdue
//     University Corny News Network, July 2020,
//     https://www.agry.purdue.edu/ext/corn/news/timeless/silks.html
//   Runnion: Runnion, E., "Bumble Bee Pollination in Tomato Greenhouses",
//     ENT-0092, Ohio State University Extension (Ohioline), 23 May 2023,
//     https://ohioline.osu.edu/factsheet/ent-0092
//   Hanna: Benedict, L.F., reporting research by Hanna, H.Y., "Pollinating
//     Greenhouse Tomatoes with Vibrators, Blowers", Louisiana Agriculture,
//     winter 2005, LSU AgCenter,
//     https://www.lsuagcenter.com/portals/communications/publications/agmag/archive/2005/winter/pollinatinggreenhousetomatoeswithvibratorsblowers
//   Seeds of Diversity: "Greenhouse Sweet and Hot Peppers (Capsicum annuum)",
//     Pollinator Best Practices, Seeds of Diversity Canada (no date shown),
//     https://seeds.ca/pollinator/bestpractices/greenhouse_peppers.html
//   Suza and Lamkey: Suza, W. and Lamkey, K. (eds.), "Crop Improvement",
//     Iowa State University Digital Press, chapter 1 "Basic Principles of
//     Plant Breeding" (LibreTexts copy, page dated 15 July 2023),
//     https://bio.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Agriculture_and_Horticulture/Crop_Improvement_(Suza_and_Lamkey)/01:_Chapters/1.01:_Basic_Principles_of_Plant_Breeding
//   ICRISAT: Sajja, S.B., Samineni, S. and Gaur, P.M., "Botany of Chickpea",
//     in Varshney, R.K. et al. (eds.), The Chickpea Genome, Springer, 2017,
//     https://oar.icrisat.org/10476/1/Botany%20of%20Chickpea.pdf
//   Saskatchewan: "Red Lentils", Pulse Crops, Government of Saskatchewan
//     (no date shown),
//     https://www.saskatchewan.ca/business/agriculture-natural-resources-and-industry/agribusiness-farmers-and-ranchers/crops-and-irrigation/field-crops/pulse-crop-bean-chickpea-faba-bean-lentils/red-lentils
//   Hilli: Hilli, H.J., Kapoor, R. and Amandeep, "Oat Floral Biology and
//     Factors Affecting Seed Set", Bhartiya Krishi Anushandhan Patrika,
//     accepted June 2021,
//     https://arccarticles.s3.amazonaws.com/arcc/Attachment-at-accept-article-BKAP275.pdf
//   Koppert: Koppert, "Natupol Smart" product page (no date shown),
//     https://www.koppert.com/natupol-smart/; the same "Hygiene" line is on
//     "Natupol" (https://www.koppert.com/natupol/) and "Natupol Excel"
//     (https://www.koppert.com/natupol-excel/). Read 2026-09-27.
//   Arbico: Arbico Organics, "Natupol Bumblebee Hives" product page (no date
//     shown), https://www.arbico-organics.com/product/natupol-bumblebee-hives/pollination-aids.
//     Read 2026-09-27.
//   Hochmuth: Hochmuth, G.J. and Hochmuth, R.C., "Production of Greenhouse
//     Tomatoes - Florida Greenhouse Vegetable Production Handbook, Vol 3",
//     HS788, University of Florida IFAS Extension, an archival copy (EDIS
//     record dated 1 January 2012), https://doi.org/10.32473/edis-cv266-1990.
//     Read 2026-09-27.
//   Hanna 2004: Hanna, H.Y., "Air Blowers Are Less Effective Pollinators of
//     Greenhouse Tomatoes than Electric Vibrators but Cost Less to Operate",
//     HortTechnology 14(1): 104-107, January 2004,
//     https://doi.org/10.21273/HORTTECH.14.1.0104 (the abstract, read
//     2026-09-27)
(
    hive_cover_m2: 1000.0,
    colony_life_days: 70.0,
    colony_item: "bumblebee_colony_0",
    // THE CIRCULATING FAN. What real indoor growers do at high growth
    // speeds, where hand pollination every garden day is impractical, was
    // looked for crop by crop (2026-09-27); only one fan result is
    // measured.
    //   STRAWBERRY: sets 0.79. McGregor (strawberry chapter), Allen and Gaede
    //   (1963) in the greenhouse: plants "uncaged and undisturbed set 20
    //   percent; those uncaged but receiving wind from a fan over them set 77
    //   percent; whereas those that were caged, but brush pollinated daily,
    //   set 97 percent of the flowers"; 77 / 97 = 0.79 of the brushed set
    //   (strawberry's no_help, below, is the same study's 20 / 97).
    //   TOMATO, PEPPER, EGGPLANT: not helped; no source measures a fan on
    //   them indoors, and the sources that speak to it say a room's air is
    //   not enough. Hochmuth: "Outdoors, wind assists in pollen dehiscence,
    //   but in the greenhouse, the flowers must be vibrated." What works for
    //   tomato is air aimed at the flower clusters, by hand, and less well
    //   than a vibrator (Hanna 2004: "Vibrator-pollinated plants of each
    //   cultivar produced greater marketable yield than did blower-pollinated
    //   plants"): that is the Hand-pollinate action, not a fan. The labour-
    //   free answer growers built for tomato is an automatic vibrator on the
    //   trellis cable (Hochmuth: "These problems have caused some growers to
    //   devise automatic vibrating systems in the house that vibrate the whole
    //   trellis system", "operated by a time clock. Vibration can be set for a
    //   few seconds (5 to 10) daily"); the home's tomatoes grow in tower cups,
    //   not on a trellis, and no power draw for such a vibrator was found, so
    //   it is not in the game yet. For eggplant McGregor (eggplant chapter) is
    //   plain: "Wind is not a factor in eggplant pollination, and vibration of
    //   the blossom will not cause a sufficient deposit of pollen on the
    //   stigma." For all three, the bumblebee colony is the sourced answer.
    // The fan itself (its size and draw) is the catalog's "circulation_fan"
    // in data/machines/home.ron.
    devices: [
        (
            machine: "circulation_fan",
            name: "circulating fan",
            sets: { "strawberry": 0.79 },
        ),
    ],
    crops: [
        // TOMATO. McGregor (tomato chapter): "Various tests in greenhouses
        // have proven that the tomato flower is not self-pollinating. However,
        // if the inflorescence is shaken, the pollen will fall from the anthers
        // onto the stigma and fertilization will result." Hanna: "In the
        // absence of naturally occurring wind in the greenhouse, tomato flowers
        // have to be vibrated by some mechanical means, such as electric
        // vibrators, air blowers or bumblebees, to release the pollen."
        // no_help 0.49: McGregor, "Moore (1968) obtained only 4.3 pounds of
        //   fruit from control plants in a plastic greenhouse; 6.6 pounds, from
        //   hormone treated plants; 8.8 pounds, from vibrator treated plants";
        //   4.3 / 8.8 = 0.49. The published range is wide, and this sits inside
        //   it: UMD Tunnels, "just 1-2 visits by bumblebees to tomato flowers
        //   will result in greater than 80% fruit set vs no visits which result
        //   in approximately 30% fruit set" (30 / 80 = 0.375 of the flowers),
        //   while in Abak's glasshouses the bee-pollinated plants yielded only
        //   17% more than netted ones (1 / 1.17 = 0.85). Moore's figure is a
        //   yield, which is what the harvest counts, and sits between the two.
        // hand_every_days 2.5: McGregor, "For maximum effectiveness, vibration
        //   must be repeated every 2 or 3 days."
        (
            id: "tomato",
            by: Vibration,
            flowering: ["flower"],
            no_help: 0.49,
            hand_every_days: 2.5,
            how: "tap each flower truss, or hold an electric toothbrush to its stem for a second, so the pollen falls",
        ),
        // PEPPER. Seeds of Diversity: "Pepper flowers are self-fertile, and
        // most flowers can set fruit without cross-pollination." But: "In
        // windless greenhouses, insect activity is generally required to
        // facilitate both self- and cross-pollination in pepper crops."
        // no_help 0.65: McGregor (pepper chapter), "Erwin (1932) measured the
        //   effect of pollination on set of fruit. He found that only 46
        //   percent of self-pollinated flowers set compared to 71 percent that
        //   were left to open pollination by bees"; 46 / 71 = 0.65.
        // hand_every_days 1: the same chapter, on the stigma: "apparently it
        //   functions only the first day the flower opens", so each day's new
        //   flowers need their own visit.
        (
            id: "pepper",
            by: Vibration,
            flowering: ["flower"],
            no_help: 0.65,
            hand_every_days: 1.0,
            how: "touch each newly opened flower with an electric toothbrush or a soft brush, the day it opens",
        ),
        // EGGPLANT. no_help 0.81: Abak, bumblebees in a glasshouse against
        //   netted control plots: "In the bumble bee pollinated plants, yield
        //   was higher than in the control plants (23% in eggplant, 17% in
        //   tomato)"; 1 / 1.23 = 0.81. The range runs to nothing: McGregor
        //   (eggplant chapter) cites Jones and Rosa (1928), "plants grown in a
        //   screened house isolated from insects were nonfruitful". Today's
        //   cultivars in Abak's trial are the better guide to what a home grows.
        // hand_every_days 2.5: GAME ESTIMATE. No source gives an interval for
        //   eggplant; it is buzz-pollinated like the tomato (same family, the
        //   same pore-opening anthers), so the tomato's is used.
        (
            id: "eggplant",
            by: Vibration,
            flowering: ["flower"],
            no_help: 0.81,
            hand_every_days: 2.5,
            how: "tap the flower clusters, or touch them with an electric toothbrush, so the pollen falls",
        ),
        // CUCUMBER. McGregor (cucumber chapter): "Numerous tests have shown
        // that all present varieties of cucumber are inter-fertile, but the
        // pollen must be transferred to the stigma by a pollinating agent,
        // usually honey bees. The exception is the previously mentioned
        // parthenocarpic slicing cultivars." A seedless (parthenocarpic)
        // greenhouse cucumber would be its own plants.csv row with no_help 1;
        // the row here is the ordinary garden cucumber.
        // no_help 0: with the pollen never carried, the female flower sets
        //   nothing. UMD Vegetables: "Crops in the plant family Cucurbitaceae
        //   such as cucumber, muskmelon/cantaloupe, squash, pumpkin, and
        //   watermelon require cross-pollination to produce fruits."
        // hand_every_days 1: UF/IFAS, of the squash family's flowers,
        //   "individual flowers are only open for a few hours in the morning,
        //   typically lasting until around noon", so each morning's flowers
        //   need that morning's visit.
        (
            id: "cucumber",
            by: Insects,
            flowering: ["flower"],
            no_help: 0.0,
            hand_every_days: 1.0,
            how: "each morning, carry pollen from a male flower to a female one (the one with a tiny fruit behind it) with a soft brush, or pick the male flower and touch it to her",
        ),
        // ZUCCHINI, SQUASH and PUMPKIN (Cucurbita). McGregor (pumpkin, squash
        // and gourd chapter): "Because the anthers are in one flower and the
        // stigma is in another, the mechanical transfer of pollen is essential
        // to fruit set." UF/IFAS: "All varieties of squash and pumpkin are
        // highly dependent on insect pollination, without which crop yields
        // are low to nonexistent."
        // no_help 0: the "nonexistent" end, for a home with no insect at all.
        //   (A few zucchini cultivars set some fruit unpollinated; they would
        //   be their own rows.)
        // hand_every_days 1: UF/IFAS, flowers "only open for a few hours in
        //   the morning".
        (
            id: "zucchini",
            by: Insects,
            flowering: ["flower"],
            no_help: 0.0,
            hand_every_days: 1.0,
            how: "each morning, carry pollen from a male flower to a female one (the one with a tiny fruit behind it) with a soft brush, or pick the male flower and touch it to her",
        ),
        (
            id: "squash",
            by: Insects,
            flowering: ["flower"],
            no_help: 0.0,
            hand_every_days: 1.0,
            how: "each morning, carry pollen from a male flower to a female one (the one with a tiny fruit behind it) with a soft brush, or pick the male flower and touch it to her",
        ),
        (
            id: "pumpkin",
            by: Insects,
            flowering: ["flower"],
            no_help: 0.0,
            hand_every_days: 1.0,
            how: "each morning, carry pollen from a male flower to a female one (the one with a tiny fruit behind it) with a soft brush, or pick the male flower and touch it to her",
        ),
        // WATERMELON. McGregor (watermelon chapter): "Watermelon pollen is not
        // windblown (Porter 1931). The flowers are almost exclusively insect
        // pollinated." no_help 0 and hand_every_days 1 as the other cucurbits.
        // (plants.csv has no melon row yet; a muskmelon would be the same:
        // McGregor reports muskmelon plants "shielded from bees, set no fruit".)
        (
            id: "watermelon",
            by: Insects,
            flowering: ["flower"],
            no_help: 0.0,
            hand_every_days: 1.0,
            how: "each morning, carry pollen from a male flower to a female one (the one with a tiny melon behind it) with a soft brush",
        ),
        // STRAWBERRY. McGregor (strawberry chapter): "Allen and Gaede (1963)
        // studied fruit-setting of 'Shasta' strawberries in the greenhouse and
        // showed that plants caged and undisturbed by man, insects, or breezes
        // set no fruit; those uncaged and undisturbed set 20 percent; those
        // uncaged but receiving wind from a fan over them set 77 percent;
        // whereas those that were caged, but brush pollinated daily, set 97
        // percent of the flowers."
        // no_help 0.21: the uncaged, undisturbed greenhouse plants, 20 / 97.
        //   Outdoors, self-pollination does better (Klatt: "Bee-pollinated
        //   fruits were on average 11.0% heavier than wind-pollinated and 30.3%
        //   heavier than self-pollinated fruits", so 0.77 by weight), and a
        //   still, sealed room does worse (the caged plants set none).
        // hand_every_days 1: Allen and Gaede's brush, "daily".
        (
            id: "strawberry",
            by: Insects,
            flowering: ["flower"],
            no_help: 0.21,
            hand_every_days: 1.0,
            how: "brush the centre of each open flower with a soft paintbrush every day, flower to flower",
        ),
        // SUNFLOWER. NDSU: "Current hybrids have been selected for and possess
        // high levels of self-compatibility. However, modern hybrids benefit
        // from insect pollination." and "Scientific literature indicates that
        // yield could increase as much as 48.8% and oil percentage could
        // increase 6.4% in bee-exposed hybrids."
        // no_help 0.67: 1 / 1.488, the largest loss NDSU reports for today's
        //   hybrids. Older cultivars lost far more: McGregor (sunflower
        //   chapter), Luttso (1956), "Caged without bees, 315 g; exposed to
        //   bees, 995 g" of seed from ten heads (0.32).
        // hand_every_days 2: McGregor, the florets "are normally open 2 or
        //   more days" and "The opening of all the florets of a single head
        //   takes 5 to 10 days", so a head rubbed every other day reaches each
        //   ring of florets while it is open. McGregor also reports the method:
        //   "supplemental pollination of the heads with a soft mitten".
        (
            id: "sunflower",
            by: Insects,
            flowering: ["bloom"],
            no_help: 0.67,
            hand_every_days: 2.0,
            how: "rub the ring of open florets on each head with a soft glove or cloth, and move between heads",
        ),
        // CORN. UMD Vegetables: "Corn is pollinated by wind and should be
        // planted in blocks for adequate cross-pollination to occur." Indoors
        // there is no wind; the pollen only falls straight down.
        // no_help 0.5: GAME ESTIMATE. No source measured corn in still air;
        //   a tassel stands above its own ear, so some pollen reaches its
        //   silks by falling, and the rest of each ear goes unfilled. Bees do
        //   nothing for it (by: Wind), only a hand does.
        // hand_every_days 3: GAME ESTIMATE from Purdue: "Silks remain receptive
        //   to pollen grain germination up to 10 days after silk emergence, but
        //   to an ever-decreasing degree." and "The majority of successful ovule
        //   fertilization occurs during the first 4 to 5 days after silk
        //   emergence", so shaking every third day reaches each silk well
        //   inside its best days.
        (
            id: "corn",
            by: Wind,
            flowering: ["silk"],
            no_help: 0.5,
            hand_every_days: 3.0,
            how: "shake the tassels over the silks, or collect pollen in a paper bag and sprinkle it on them",
        ),
        // SELF-POLLINATING: these set fully indoors with no help, listed so
        // the crop card can say so with a source.
        //
        // BEAN. McGregor (bean chapter): "Bean breeders state that beans
        // excluded from insect visitors set a full complement of pods and
        // seeds", and Taylor (1919) "stated that the flowers are entirely
        // self-fertile as is abundantly proved by their productiveness when
        // grown in greenhouses where bees are not present."
        (id: "bean", by: SelfPollinating, no_help: 1.0),
        // PEA. McGregor (introduction) lists "beans, cotton, flax, peanuts,
        // peas, and soybeans" as crops that "may receive some benefit from
        // insect pollination but are largely self-pollinating". Suza and
        // Lamkey: "Cleistogamy is observed in some legumes (e.g., groundnut,
        // peas, some beans, soybean)." (cleistogamy: the pollen is shed before
        // the flower opens).
        (id: "pea", by: SelfPollinating, no_help: 1.0),
        // SOYBEAN. McGregor (soybean chapter): "Tests with plants caged to
        // exclude bees have shown no decrease in production over exposed
        // plots".
        (id: "soybean", by: SelfPollinating, no_help: 1.0),
        // CHICKPEA. ICRISAT: "Chickpea is a highly self-pollinated crop. The
        // anthers dehisce one day before the flower opens ensuring
        // self-pollination."
        (id: "chickpea", by: SelfPollinating, no_help: 1.0),
        // LENTIL. Saskatchewan: "Flowers are self-pollinated."
        (id: "lentil", by: SelfPollinating, no_help: 1.0),
        // OKRA. McGregor (okra chapter): "Okra is self-fertile" and "Purewal
        // and Randhawa (1947) reported that 100 percent of both bagged and
        // open flowers set fruit".
        (id: "okra", by: SelfPollinating, no_help: 1.0),
        // WHEAT, RICE and BARLEY. Suza and Lamkey: "In some cereals (e.g.,
        // rice, wheat, and barley) the majority of self-pollination occurs
        // before flowers open", and "Examples of important crops with an
        // autogamous mating system include: sorghum, millet, rice, wheat,
        // barley".
        (id: "wheat", by: SelfPollinating, no_help: 1.0),
        (id: "rice", by: SelfPollinating, no_help: 1.0),
        (id: "barley", by: SelfPollinating, no_help: 1.0),
        // OAT. Hilli: "It's a self-pollinated crop with a limited range of
